/-!
# Theorem 3.5's instances without a named `Prop`: ergodicity of affine moves by Fourier analysis

`theo:universality_tori` — `main.tex:291` (the minimal moves `main.tex:293`, the RL moves
`main.tex:440`, the IL moves `main.tex:493`, the figure `main.tex:1022`, the limitations
`main.tex:697–698`; Appendix B.2 `main.tex:808–821`). Not marked: the printed theorem is false
(C-31: a model whose heads ignore the state is never ε-universal,
`EGF.not_isUniversalFamilyEps_constOutflow`); this file discharges the ergodicity hypothesis of its
corrected form `EGF.universality_tori_of_ergodic` (C-28 tori half, C-29) for the paper's instances.

> Any affine toroidal family is a universal EGF family provided mild technical assumptions on the
> $(\Phi_i)_{i=1}^p$. (`main.tex:291`)

> We develop a theory of EGFs, presenting a universality criterion and demonstrating that, in any
> dimension on tori and spheres, this criterion can be fulfilled with four simple transformations.
> (`main.tex:149`)

> An EGF is built with the minimal four affine transformations described in section
> \ref{sec:universality_tori}, implemented as MLPs with 3 hidden layers of width 32.
> (`main.tex:493`)

> The ``technical assumptions" above is in particular satisfied if the group generated by the
> linear part of the $\Phi_i$ is the whole group $\mathrm{SL}_d(\mathbb Z)$. It is shown in
> \citet{conder2025generating} that $\mathrm{SL}_d(\mathbb Z)$ has a presentation with two
> generators. Therefore, in any dimension, EGF are universal with $p=4$ and well-chosen
> $(\Phi_i)_{i=1}^4$: the two generators and their inverse. (`main.tex:293`)

> An EGF on $\Statespace = \mathbb T^2$ is built with 16 transformations (8 translations and 2
> elements of $\mathrm{SL}_d(\mathbb Z)$ together with their inverse). (`main.tex:440`)

> Theoretically minimal EGF with four affine moves on the torus $\mathbb T^2$ ie two generators
> of $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb Z)$ together with their inverse. (`main.tex:1022`)

> (for instance on tori, one randomly initialized non-trainable translation is sufficient to
> ensure ergodicity) (`main.tex:697`)

## What is proved

| | |
|---|---|
| `mFourier_comp_torusAffine`, `norm_mFourierCoeff_eq_of_invariant`, `ergodic_of_mFourierCoeff` | `e_k(Ax + b) = e_k(b) e_{Aᵀk}(x)`; invariant `f` has `‖f̂(Aᵀk)‖ = ‖f̂(k)‖`; Fourier uniqueness and Parseval for the global `volume`, hence an ergodicity criterion |
| `NoPeriodicVector`, `noPeriodicVector_iff_det`, `ergodic_torusAffine_of_noPeriodic` | an affine map whose linear part has no periodic integer vector (`det(Aⁿ − 1) ≠ 0`, `n ≥ 1`) is ergodic, for every translation |
| `ergodic_torusAffine_one_of_mFourier_ne_one`, `ae_ergodic_torusAffine_one`, `ergodic_torusAffine_one_dim_one` | Kronecker: `x ↦ x + c` is ergodic if `e_k(c) ≠ 1` for `k ≠ 0`, true for a.e. `c`; on `𝕋¹` for `c` of infinite order |
| `isGroupErgodic_of_ergodic_mem` | one ergodic element makes the group ergodic (C-29) |
| `catMat`, `matT_mul_matS_mul_inv`, `noPeriodicVector_catMat`, `mem_torusAffineGroup_commutator` | `[[2,1],[1,1]] = T S T⁻¹ S⁻¹` has no periodic vector; for any translations `Φ_T Φ_S Φ_T⁻¹ Φ_S⁻¹ = x ↦ catMat x + c` is in the group of the moves |
| `plasticMat`, `exists_noPeriodicVector_det_one` | every `SL_d(ℤ)`, `d ≥ 2`, has an element without periodic vector (blocks of `catMat` and the companion of `x³ − x − 1`) |
| `universality_tori_of_mem_noPeriodic`, `universality_tori_of_mem_translation` | such an element in the group of the moves, a rich model ⇒ ε-universal |
| `universality_tori_minimal`, `universality_tori_rl`, `universality_tori_cat`, `exists_universality_tori_single_move`, `ae_universality_tori_translation` | the minimal four moves, the RL moves (both readings of C-34), one move in every `d ≥ 2`, one random translation in every `d`: ε-universal for every choice of translations |
| `universality_tori_sl`, `minimalMoves_generate`, `not_universality_tori_sl_one` | l.293 for every `d ≥ 2`; the minimal moves meet it; it fails at `d = 1` |
| `universality_tori_one`, `universality_tori_one_sqrt_two` | C-28 (b): on `𝕋¹` one translation of infinite order suffices |

## Hypothesis checklist

| paper hypothesis (`main.tex:291`, `:293`, `:820`) | here (`universality_tori_minimal`, `universality_tori_sl`) |
|---|---|
| `Φᵢ ∈ Aff(𝕋^d)`, linear parts in `SL_d(ℤ)` | ⚠ weakened: `IsUnit (A i).det` (`GL_d(ℤ)`) |
